<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Splash wordmark fix (2026-08-28): replaces the 5 raster drawable-{m,h,x,xx,xxx}dpi/
  splash_branding.png files the "Add splash screen app name" commit (f0ef79d, feat/feed-visit-ux)
  introduced. Those PNGs were Pillow-rendered "TerraWatch" in Arial Bold at ~93x17dp (see that
  commit's own message) - wrong brand font (not Inter) AND, per direct user report, visibly
  pixelated/jaggy on a real device once the OS scales a small raster bitmap for
  windowSplashScreenBrandingImage. A VectorDrawable sidesteps both: Android rasterizes path data
  fresh at whatever exact pixel size the splash-screen branding slot needs, at every density, so
  there is no upscale-a-small-bitmap step left to go jaggy, and this is real Inter SemiBold glyph
  geometry rather than a font-substitution guess.

  SLOT-SIZE FIX (2026-09-06), from a real user report that the splash wordmark looked stretched:
  this drawable used to be 184x24dp, its exact glyph bounding box. Android's branded-image slot is
  a FIXED 200x80dp, and the OS scales whatever it is given to fill that slot - so a 7.65:1 drawable
  in a 2.5:1 slot was stretched vertically by about 3.3x. The letterforms went tall and narrow; see
  docs/qa/android-platform-17/splash-before-184x24-stretched.png next to -after-200x80.png.

  The glyph path data below is UNCHANGED. Only the frame around it moved: the drawable is now
  declared at exactly the slot's 200x80dp, viewportHeight was widened from 1513 to 4631.6 so the
  viewport matches the slot's 2.5:1 ratio, and the single <group android:translateY> centres the
  original glyph box inside that taller viewport. Same aspect ratio as the font, no scaling by the
  OS at all, and the wordmark renders about 26dp tall - close to the 24dp originally intended.

  HOW THIS WAS GENERATED - not hand-drawn, not traced: the string "TerraWatch" was laid out and
  its outlines extracted programmatically from this repo's own
  core/ui/src/commonMain/composeResources/font/inter_semibold.ttf via fontTools (glyf outlines
  per character, walked left-to-right using each glyph's own hmtx advance width - no GPOS kerning
  applied; Inter's default spacing reads fine at this length, same call the task brief made).
  Each glyph's outline was emitted through fontTools' SVGPathPen behind a single TransformPen
  carrying (a,b,c,d,e,f) = (1,0,0,-1, x_cursor-min_x, max_y) - this both flips the font's Y-up/
  baseline-origin coordinate space into the Y-down/top-left-origin space pathData below uses, AND
  shifts every glyph into its left-to-right position, in one affine step, so nothing downstream
  needed a <group> transform. The ten glyphs' path strings are simply concatenated (each is its
  own M...Z subpath(s); android:fillType defaults to nonZero, which is what a TrueType font's own
  contour winding is designed to satisfy for the counters in "e"/"a"/"c"/"h" etc). viewportWidth/
  Height below are the EXACT tight bounding box of the composed word in the font's native
  2048-units-per-em space (no rounding) - verified against a from-scratch decode of the first
  glyph's own path ("T": crossbar 1186x225 sitting on a 267-wide stem down to baseline at
  y=1490, both exactly where a capital T's real metrics should put them) before trusting the rest.

  Fill: core/ui's Tokens.kt TerraColors.Ink (#17222E) - same "hex copied here deliberately, plain
  XML can't reference the Kotlin object" precedent this app's other splash/launcher assets already
  use (see values/themes.xml and ic_launcher_foreground.xml's own header comments).

  Size: android:width/height (184x24dp) keep the SAME aspect ratio as the extracted glyph geometry
  (11579x1513 native units, ratio 7.653; 184/24 = 7.667, a 0.2% mismatch from rounding to whole
  dp - imperceptible). 24dp is a deliberate, modest bump from the old PNGs' ~17dp (mdpi) height,
  not a redesign - Inter's proportions differ slightly from the old Arial-Bold render and needed
  *some* adjustment, but the brief was to fix pixelation/font, not resize the brand mark, so this
  stayed close to the original visual weight rather than maximizing toward the platform's
  documented ~200x80dp windowSplashScreenBrandingImage cap (184dp width still clears that cap with
  16dp/8% margin either way).
-->
